.TH XUDO 1 "2026-11-07" "xudo 0.1.0-beta.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
xudo \- decide whether a privileged command runs as root
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xudo
.RI [ options ]
.I command
.RI [ args ]
.br
.B xudo explain
.RB [ \-\-action
.IR id ]
.RB [ options ]
.B \-\-
.I program
.RI [ arg ...]
.br
.B xudo why\-not
.B \-\-action
.I id
.RB [ options ]
.B \-\-
.I program
.RI [ arg ...]
.br
.B xudo exec
.RB [ options ]
.B \-\-
.I program
.RI [ arg ...]
.br
.B xudo check
.RB [ options ]
.br
.B xudo list
.RB [ \-\-tier
.IR tier ]
.RB [ options ]
.br
.B xudo doctor
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-fix ]
.RB [ options ]
.br
.B xudo config
.RB [ \-\-schema ]
.RB [ options ]
.br
.B xudo install
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-\-show\-contents ]
.RB [ \-\-uninstall ]
.RB [ options ]
.br
.B xudo audit
.RB [ \-\-log
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-\-against\-journal ]
.RB [ options ]
.br
.B xudo agentdoc
.RB [ \-\-format
.IR harness ]
.RB [ options ]
.br
.B xudo help
.RI [ command ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xudo
checks a request against root-owned rules.
.B xudo exec
runs an allowed command as root through the host's
.B sudo
or
.BR doas ,
which asks for your password.
This beta is that password path only.
The passwordless agent path is not built: the package has no
.B xudo\-gate\-nopass
and installs no
.B NOPASSWD
rule.
.PP
This release is a beta and has not been externally audited.
.SH OPTIONS
These options apply to every command.
They may come before or after the command name.
.TP
.BI \-\-rules\  DIR
Directory of rule files.
The default is
.IR /etc/xudo/rules.d .
.TP
.BI \-\-config\-file\  FILE
The settings file.
The default is
.IR /etc/xudo/config.toml .
.TP
.BI \-\-output\  FORMAT
.B human
(the default) prints prose and the decision trace.
.B json
prints the versioned decision envelope.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help.
.B \-h
is the short form.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
The version line says the build is a beta and not externally audited.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B explain
Show what the policy would decide, and why.
Executes nothing.
The command is a real argument vector after
.BR \-\- ;
the program is the first argument.
.B \-\-action
asks about one action id.
Every other action that accepts the same command still applies, and
overlapping actions resolve to the strictest.
.TP
.B why\-not
Show why one action would refuse this argv.
.B \-\-action
is required.
Executes nothing.
.TP
.B exec
Run the command as root, if the broker permits it.
Asks for your password through the host backend.
The program is an absolute path, after
.BR \-\- .
The command's own status is the process status.
A policy refusal happens before the command runs.
If a human watch approval is pending, the default timeout returns at once
with exit status 18 and leaves the request pending.
Set
.B confirmation_timeout_secs
in the settings file to wait for approval; it defaults to 0 and accepts values
from 0 through 600 seconds. The settings file,
.IR /etc/xudo/config.toml ,
must be readable by the invoking user; otherwise exec fails closed with status
19. A positive timeout of n seconds starts after the broker first reports the
pending request. If the broker is silent, xudo waits at most n+5 seconds from
that point. After an approval, the consuming run request has a separate maximum
of 5 seconds. With
.B \-\-output json,
each pending update is one compact JSON object per line (JSON Lines).
.TP
.B watch
Watch for pending confirmation requests on this terminal.
The broker checks the watcher's session and terminal on each heartbeat and
answer. A line shows the canonical command, governing rule, classification,
evidence, time left, and the action's final word. Type that word to approve,
or
.B n
to deny. Approval still requires the command's password. Input typed before
the prompt is flushed; a rejected answer leaves the request pending.
.TP
.B pending
List this user's pending confirmation requests without answering them.
This command does not require a terminal.
.TP
.B freeze
Requires
.BR \-\-reason \fITEXT\fR .
Freeze the agent path with an operator supplied reason. Until agent
enrollment ships (M4) this sets the flag but changes nothing for new requests
or for human requests: every caller is unattested, and the human password path
stays available. Pending or approved requests of an enrolled agent are denied
and audited, without a backoff strike; a human's pending request is left alone.
.TP
.B thaw
Remove the freeze marker and resume the agent path. Accepted only from uid 0;
run it as
.BR "sudo xudo thaw" .
.TP
.B check
Lint the loaded ruleset.
Exits
.B 17
when an action allowlists an executable the compiled-in deny-list refuses.
Overlapping actions are reported and are not themselves a failure: a command
more than one action accepts is refused to an agent if any of them is
human-only, and confirmed as strictly as the strictest demands.
A ruleset that will not load is
.BR 19 ,
before this lint runs.
.TP
.B list
List each action's id, tier, executable, and the file and line it came from.
.B \-\-tier
shows actions whose tier contains that name.
Hyphens in the name are ignored, so
.B diag\-read
matches.
The tier names are
.BR diag\-read ,
.BR svc\-control ,
.BR pkg\-install ,
.BR pkg\-remove ,
.BR sys\-tunable ,
.BR net\-filter ,
.BR critical .
No match exits
.BR 17 .
.TP
.B doctor
Report the platform, the ruleset, how this caller would be classified, and
every other path to root on this machine.
It reads as you.
What you cannot read is reported as unverified, never as absent; run it as
root as well to read what this account cannot.
.B \-\-json
prints the versioned machine-readable envelope, the same as
.BR \-\-output\ json .
.B \-\-fix
proposes a fix for each path to root, including the command a human would
run, and applies nothing.
.TP
.B config
Show the effective settings and where each value came from.
.B \-\-schema
prints the machine-readable schema instead.
Only
.B \-\-config\-file
is read, as the system layer.
A missing file is the defaults, not an error.
A file that does not parse, or that contains an unrecognised key, exits
.BR 19 .
The keys are
.B confirm_agent_actions
(default true),
.B confirm_human_path
(default false),
.B confirmation_timeout_secs
(default 0, maximum 600 seconds),
.B frozen
(default false),
.B audit_sink
(default
.BR journald ),
.B color
(default true), and
.BR use_pty ,
which is compiled in as true and is refused if set.
When enabled, a rule that requires confirmation also requires an out-of-band
watch approval; the password alone does not satisfy it.
The command marks security-relevant settings and says they are never
settable from the environment.
This binary does not read the environment for them.
.TP
.B install
Integrate xudo with the local
.B sudo
or
.B doas
backend, or remove that integration.
Needs root.
Every step is validated by the backend's own parser before anything is moved,
and only files xudo wrote are replaced or removed.
.B \-\-dry\-run
prints the plan and changes nothing.
.B \-\-show\-contents
prints the file each step would write.
.B \-\-uninstall
removes what an install wrote, for both backends, and nothing else.
The rules this build would install all require a password.
An install is reported as verified only when the broker minted a request for
.BR "id -u" ,
the gate ran through the backend and printed
.BR 0 ,
and the backend lists the gate for a member of the group.
Anything less keeps the validated files, says
.BR "NOT VERIFIED" ,
and exits
.BR 19 .
.TP
.B audit
Verify that a hash-chained log has not been edited in place.
The default path is
.IR $HOME/.local/state/ido/log.jsonl .
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
is not read; if it is set, pass
.BR \-\-log .
The broker's own log is
.IR /var/lib/xudo/audit.jsonl .
.B \-\-against\-journal
compares file records with the journal's root-written xudo entries, which
can show a tail removed or records replaced after a cut.
An unbroken chain does not prove the log was never truncated and rebuilt.
A damaged log exits
.BR 17 .
A log that cannot be read, or a journal check that cannot run, exits
.BR 19 .
.TP
.B agentdoc
Print the block that tells an agent what the loaded rules permit.
.B \-\-format
is
.B agents
(the default,
.BR AGENTS.md ),
.B claude
.RB ( CLAUDE.md ),
or
.B codex
(Codex CLI).
.TP
.B help
Print the same text as
.BR \-\-help ,
or the help of the named command.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The request succeeded.
.B exec
returns the command's own status instead, so a command that exits 0 is the
only successful run; any other status from the command, or from the password
prompt, is that status.
.TP
.B 17
Denied by policy, or an audit log that is not intact.
.TP
.B 18
Confirmation is required, or there is no live confirmation channel.
Both mean nobody has approved the action.
An approval request still pending at the configured timeout also exits 18.
.TP
.B 19
.B xudo
could not operate and failed closed: the ruleset or the settings file would
not load, no usable backend, an install that is not verified, an audit log
that could not be read, or a status that does not fit in a byte.
.PP
.BR 17 ,
.BR 18 ,
and
.B 19
sit above the
.BR 0 / 1 / 2
band a program uses for its own failures and below
.BR 128 ,
where a shell reports a killed child as
.IR 128+signal .
A refusal is therefore distinct from a command that ran and failed, and from
a command that was killed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/xudo/rules.d/
Rule files, loaded in name order.
See
.BR xudo\-rules (5).
.TP
.I /etc/xudo/config.toml
Settings.
Not a rule file.
.TP
.I /run/xudo/broker.sock
The broker socket.
systemd listens; one
.B xudod
serves each connection.
Mode
.BR 0660 ,
group
.BR xudo .
.TP
.I /var/lib/xudo/audit.jsonl
The hash-chained audit log the broker and the gate append to.
.TP
.I /etc/sudoers.d/xudo
Written by
.BR "xudo install" ,
mode
.BR 0440 .
Not shipped in the package.
The name has no dot and no trailing tilde, which
.B sudo
would ignore.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/xudod.socket
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/xudod@.service
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/xudo-pasban.service
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/xudo-pasban.timer
The broker socket, one broker instance per connection, and the daily audit
check.
Pasban runs
.B xudo audit \-\-log /var/lib/xudo/audit.jsonl \-\-against\-journal
as root.
.SH SECURITY
Agent detection is not a security boundary.
.BR CLAUDECODE ,
.BR argv[0] ,
.BR comm ,
the command line,
.BR /proc/ pid /exe ,
and process ancestry are unauthenticated hints.
They are recorded.
Authorization does not branch on them.
An unattested caller takes the human path and is asked for a password.
.PP
Confirmation is not read from the requester's standard input, standard
output, or terminal.
No live confirmation channel means deny.
.PP
This beta has not been externally audited.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xudo\-rules (5),
.BR ido (1),
.BR sudo (8),
.BR doas (1)
